    "manifest": {
      "title": "Zero velocity at the top is not zero acceleration",
      "summary": "A tossed ball rises and falls while a held ball waits on a gate that opens at the instant the tossed ball reaches its top. Velocity and acceleration arrows and readouts show that the acceleration stays -9.8 m/s^2 at the top.",
      "assumptions": [
        "Constant downward acceleration of 9.8 m/s^2, no air resistance, up is positive.",
        "An idealized vertical throw; the motion ends when the tossed ball returns to the launch mark."
      ],
      "description": "Two balls sit on one height scale with a launch line at 0 m. The tossed ball on the left rises to a marked top and falls back. The held ball on the right rests on a gate that opens at the tossed ball's top, then falls. Each ball has a velocity arrow that shrinks to a dot at zero, and a downward acceleration arrow during free flight.",
      "inputs": [
